Subject: [PATCH] mp_image: count hardware surfaces in approximate byte size

mp_image_approx_byte_size() only summed the AVBufferRefs, which for
hardware frames such as VideoToolbox hold a pointer to the surface, not its
pixels. The backward playback reversal queue, bounded by
--video-reversal-buffer, therefore never limited hardware decoding: a single
UHD GOP played backward held over 2 GB of IOSurfaces with a 1 GiB limit.
Estimate hardware frames from their frames context instead.

diff --git a/video/mp_image.c b/video/mp_image.c
index a90c127..fef76d6 100644
--- a/video/mp_image.c
+++ b/video/mp_image.c
@@ -299,6 +299,18 @@ int mp_image_approx_byte_size(struct mp_image *img)
 {
     int total = sizeof(*img);
 
+    // Hardware surfaces keep their pixels outside the AVBufferRefs (e.g. a
+    // CVPixelBuffer behind a pointer-sized buffer). Estimate them from the
+    // frames context, or queues bounded by this size, such as the backward
+    // playback reversal queue, hold unbounded amounts of decoded video.
+    if (img->hwctx) {
+        AVHWFramesContext *fctx = (void *)img->hwctx->data;
+        int fmt = pixfmt2imgfmt(fctx->sw_format);
+        int size = fmt ? mp_image_get_alloc_size(fmt, fctx->width, fctx->height, 1) : -1;
+        if (size > 0)
+            return total + size;
+    }
+
     for (int n = 0; n < MP_MAX_PLANES; n++) {
         struct AVBufferRef *buf = img->bufs[n];
         if (buf)
